<h2>Two petitions, two fates</h2>
<p>Both cases reached the Supreme Court by special leave against interim criminal orders, and both were State petitions. Their divergent outcomes were not inconsistency — they were the doctrine working as designed.</p>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><strong>Agniv Sengupta — interference.</strong> The Bombay High Court granted NDPS bail on the inference that the facility manager's access to the rented room cast doubt on the occupant's possession. The Supreme Court held the inference unsustainable — it would make every hotel-room recovery unattributable — and set the bail order aside, directing surrender within three weeks.</div>
  <div class="arrow">·</div>
  <div class="col new"><strong>Azam Khan — restraint.</strong> The Allahabad High Court stayed a conviction pending revision, citing time served, age and ill health, flight risk and the state of the evidence on forgery. The Supreme Court found those reasons valid, held there was no ground for interference under Article 136, and dismissed the State's appeal — while extending the same stay to two co-convicts on parity.</div>
</div>

<h2>The working principle</h2>
<p>Article 136 confers a discretionary, residual jurisdiction — not a further tier of appeal. Against interim orders, the Court's practice reduces to a question about the quality of the reasoning below, not the correctness of the outcome:</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>A flawed foundation invites interference.</strong> Where the entire order stands on one inference, and that inference collapses under scrutiny — as the duplicate-keys point did — the order cannot survive, however deferential the standard.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>A reasoned discretion is protected.</strong> Where the High Court weighed legitimate factors, the Supreme Court will not substitute its own balance, even if it might have struck a different one.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Parity cuts both ways.</strong> Azam Khan shows the Court using Article 136 not to curtail interim relief but to extend it — unreasoned unequal treatment of co-convicts is itself an Article 14 defect warranting correction.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Merits stay insulated.</strong> In both matters the Court ring-fenced its observations from the trial and the pending revisions.</div>
</div>

<h2>Drafting the SLP against an interim order</h2>
<p>For the petitioner, the lesson is to locate the single load-bearing error: the SLP that catalogues twenty grievances about an interim order invites dismissal in limine, while the one that isolates the inference on which everything rests — and shows it to be legally impossible — fits the Agniv Sengupta mould. For the respondent defending an interim order, the strongest shield is the order's own reasoning: a High Court order that records the factors it weighed, as the Allahabad High Court did, is very difficult to dislodge. Counsel should also frame the consequence honestly — an interim order set aside is not a final loss, and the Supreme Court's standard practice of insulating the merits should be pressed into the order itself.</p>
